From 349d8953ae0dd7ac4f0145c77465f66255ec24aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 14:44:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [DOSKUTSU] patch 0306 -- per-flip totals for the engine
 decomposition

0305 made the fine sub-buckets emit on the fixed-timestep path, but they
still could not be compared against the bucket they decompose. The
[engine-tick-stat] lines are per-CALL distribution statistics; the
engine_tick figure in the sibling audio/engine split line is a per-FLIP
average. Dividing one by the other is not a coverage fraction, and both
this session and the benchmarking session made that mistake from
opposite directions -- once concluding the sub-buckets covered under 10%
of their parent, once that they exceeded it.

Emit per-flip totals (accum/flips) ALONGSIDE the distributions, not
instead of them: the distributions answer a different question and are
what reveal a long tail. Paired per block, sum_children now matches
engine_tick to within one quantisation unit -- 3.50/3.50, 3.00/3.00,
4.40/4.40 -- so sub-bucket against parent is arithmetic instead of
inference. The answer it gives: the decomposition is exhaustive, and
mode_tick_render is ~95% of engine_tick. There is no missing residual.

The totals cannot be derived by summing the existing sample arrays.
Those hold at most g_eng_tick_block_size entries and the push is guarded
by an index bound, so any phase firing more than 100 times per block is
silently truncated -- understating exactly the busiest phases. Separate
untruncated accumulators plus real call counts, and a trunc= flag so a
reader can see when the distribution above is a subsample.

Second hunk, and the reason the first one is trustworthy: every timer
computed SDL_GetTicksNS() - t0 into an UNSIGNED value. On DOS the
timebase is uclock, which is not reliably monotonic; when it steps
backwards the subtraction wraps to ~1.8e19 ns. This was ALREADY
happening and already in the logs -- the pre-0306 clear_screen
distribution carries max=18446744073654.68 ms -- but a median over 100
samples hid it. A sum cannot, so the new totals turned one wrapped
sample into a nonsense bucket, which is how it was found. Clamped via
_nxe_diag_dt(): a backwards clock means the interval is unmeasurable,
not enormous, so dropping it biases a total slightly low while keeping
it destroys the total outright. Applied to all 27 sites in the header,
the direct accumulators included, since a sum is the most vulnerable
form.

Default-OFF behind the same FINE_INSTR gate as 0304/0305 -- verified:
zero totals lines in a default run, and the gameplay banner gate passes.
Measurement does not get to move the shipped binary's cost.

NOT fixed here, reported instead: the older phase-timing accumulators in
Renderer.cpp (dc_total / flip_inner / inter_flip) wrap the same way, ~4%
of blocks in a DOSBox reel. 0306 does not need them and campaign
readings have survived it by using medians, so it wants its own slot
rather than growing this patch while a build is waiting.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
